{"type": "tool_result", "name": "read_file", "output": "{\"content\": \"1|---\\n2|title: use client\\n3|description: Learn how to use the use client directive to render a component on the client.\\n4|---\\n5|\\n6|The `'use client'` directive declares an entry point for the components to be rendered on the **client side** and should be used when creating interactive user interfaces (UI) that require client-side JavaScript capabilities, such as state management, event handling, and access to browser APIs. This is a React feature.\\n7|\\n8|> **Good to know:**\\n9|>\\n10|> You do not need to add the `'use client'` directive to every file that contains Client Components. You only need to add it to the files whose components you want to render directly within Server Components. The `'use client'` directive defines the [server and client boundary](/docs/app/guides/server-and-client-boundary), and the components exported from such a file serve as entry points to the client.\\n11|\\n12|## Usage\\n13|\\n14|To declare an entry point for the Client Components, add the `'use client'` directive **at the top of the file**, before any imports:\\n15|\\n16|```tsx filename=\\\"app/components/counter.tsx\\\" highlight={1} switcher\\n17|'use client'\\n18|\\n19|import { useState } from 'react'\\n20|\\n21|export default function Counter() {\\n22|  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)\\n23|\\n24|  return (\\n25|    <div>\\n26|      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n27|      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n28|    </div>\\n29|  )\\n30|}\\n31|```\\n32|\\n33|```jsx filename=\\\"app/components/counter.js\\\" highlight={1} switcher\\n34|'use client'\\n35|\\n36|import { useState } from 'react'\\n37|\\n38|export default function Counter() {\\n39|  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)\\n40|\\n41|  return (\\n42|    <div>\\n43|      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n44|      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n45|    </div>\\n46|  )\\n47|}\\n48|```\\n49|\\n50|When using the `'use client'` directive, the props of the Client Components must be [serializable](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client#serializable-types). This means the props need to be in a format that React can serialize when sending data from the server to the client.\\n51|\\n52|```tsx filename=\\\"app/components/counter.tsx\\\" highlight={4} switcher\\n53|'use client'\\n54|\\n55|export default function Counter({\\n56|  onClick /* ❌ Function is not serializable */,\\n57|}) {\\n58|  return (\\n59|    <div>\\n60|      <button onClick={onClick}>Increment</button>\\n61|    </div>\\n62|  )\\n63|}\\n64|```\\n65|\\n66|```jsx filename=\\\"app/components/counter.js\\\" highlight={4} switcher\\n67|'use client'\\n68|\\n69|export default function Counter({\\n70|  onClick /* ❌ Function is not serializable */,\\n71|}) {\\n72|  return (\\n73|    <div>\\n74|      <button onClick={onClick}>Increment</button>\\n75|    </div>\\n76|  )\\n77|}\\n78|```\\n79|\\n80|## Nesting Client Components within Server Components\\n81|\\n82|Combining Server and Client Components allows you to build applications that are both performant and interactive:\\n83|\\n84|1. **Server Components**: Use for static content, data fetching, and SEO-friendly elements.\\n85|2. **Client Components**: Use for interactive elements that require state, effects, or browser APIs.\\n86|3. **Component composition**: Nest Client Components within Server Components as needed for a clear separation of server and client logic.\\n87|\\n88|In the following example:\\n89|\\n90|- `Header` is a Server Component handling static content.\\n91|- `Counter` is a Client Component enabling interactivity within the page.\\n92|\\n93|```tsx filename=\\\"app/page.tsx\\\" highlight={2,8} switcher\\n94|import Header from './header'\\n95|import Counter from './counter' // This is a Client Component\\n96|\\n97|export default function Page() {\\n98|  return (\\n99|    <div>\\n100|      <Header />\\n101|      <Counter />\\n102|    </div>\\n103|  )\\n104|}\\n105|```\\n106|\\n107|```jsx filename=\\\"app/page.js\\\" highlight={2,8} switcher\\n108|import Header from './header'\\n109|import Counter from './counter' // This is a Client Component\\n110|\\n111|export default function Page() {\\n112|  return (\\n113|    <div>\\n114|      <Header />\\n115|      <Counter />\\n116|    </div>\\n117|  )\\n118|}\\n119|```\\n120|\\n121|## Reference\\n122|\\n123|See the [React documentation](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client) for more information on `'use client'`.\", \"total_lines\": 123, \"file_size\": 3802, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false, \"not_found\": false}", "duration_ms": 13, "is_error": false, "timestamp": 1791119398012}
{"type": "tool_result", "name": "read_file", "output": "{\"content\": \"1|---\\n2|title: Server and Client Components\\n3|description: Learn how you can use React Server and Client Components to render parts of your application on the server or the client.\\n4|related:\\n5|  title: Next Steps\\n6|  description: Learn more about the APIs mentioned in this page.\\n7|  links:\\n8|    - app/api-reference/directives/use-client\\n9|    - app/guides/server-and-client-boundary\\n10|---\\n11|\\n12|By default, layouts and pages are [Server Components](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/server-components), which lets you fetch data and render parts of your UI on the server, optionally cache the result, and stream it to the client. When you need interactivity or browser APIs, you can use [Client Components](https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-client) to layer in functionality.\\n13|\\n14|This page explains how Server and Client Components work in Next.js and when to use them, with examples of how to compose them together in your application.\\n15|\\n16|> **Good to know:** For an explanation of where each component type runs and how the boundary works, see [The Server and Client Boundary](/docs/app/guides/server-and-client-boundary).\\n17|\\n18|## When to use Server and Client Components?\\n19|\\n20|The client and server environments have different capabilities. Server and Client Components allow you to run logic in each environment depending on your use case.\\n21|\\n22|Use **Client Components** when you need:\\n23|\\n24|- [State](https://react.dev/learn/managing-state) and [event handlers](https://react.dev/learn/responding-to-events). E.g. `onClick`, `onChange`.\\n25|- [Lifecycle logic](https://react.dev/learn/lifecycle-of-reactive-effects). E.g. `useEffect`.\\n26|- Browser-only APIs. E.g. `localStorage`, `window`, `navigator.geolocation`, etc.\\n27|- [Custom hooks](https://react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks).\\n28|\\n29|Use **Server Components** when you need:\\n30|\\n31|- Fetch data from databases or APIs close to the source.\\n32|- Use API keys, tokens, and other secrets without exposing them to the client.\\n33|- Reduce the amount of JavaScript sent to the browser.\\n34|- Improve the [First Contentful Paint (FCP)](https://web.dev/fcp/), and stream content progressively to the client.\\n35|\\n36|For example, the `<Page>` component is a Server Component that fetches data about a post, and passes it as props to the `<LikeButton>` which handles client-side interactivity.\\n37|\\n38|```tsx filename=\\\"app/[id]/page.tsx\\\" highlight={1,17} switcher\\n39|import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'\\n40|import { getPost } from '@/lib/data'\\n41|\\n42|export default async function Page({\\n43|  params,\\n44|}: {\\n45|  params: Promise<{ id: string }>\\n46|}) {\\n47|  const { id } = await params\\n48|  const post = await getPost(id)\\n49|\\n50|  return (\\n51|    <div>\\n52|      <main>\\n53|        <h1>{post.title}</h1>\\n54|        {/* ... */}\\n55|        <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />\\n56|      </main>\\n57|    </div>\\n58|  )\\n59|}\\n60|```\\n61|\\n62|```jsx filename=\\\"app/[id]/page.js\\\" highlight={1,13} switcher\\n63|import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'\\n64|import { getPost } from '@/lib/data'\\n65|\\n66|export default async function Page({ params }) {\\n67|  const { id } = await params\\n68|  const post = await getPost(id)\\n69|\\n70|  return (\\n71|    <div>\\n72|      <main>\\n73|        <h1>{post.title}</h1>\\n74|        {/* ... */}\\n75|        <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />\\n76|      </main>\\n77|    </div>\\n78|  )\\n79|}\\n80|```\\n81|\\n82|```tsx filename=\\\"app/ui/like-button.tsx\\\" highlight={1} switcher\\n83|'use client'\\n84|\\n85|import { useState } from 'react'\\n86|\\n87|export default function LikeButton({ likes }: { likes: number }) {\\n88|  // ...\\n89|}\\n90|```\\n91|\\n92|```jsx filename=\\\"app/ui/like-button.js\\\" highlight={1} switcher\\n93|'use client'\\n94|\\n95|import { useState } from 'react'\\n96|\\n97|export default function LikeButton({ likes }) {\\n98|  // ...\\n99|}\\n100|```\\n101|\\n102|## How do Server and Client Components work in Next.js?\\n103|\\n104|### On the server\\n105|\\n106|On the server, Next.js uses React's APIs to orchestrate rendering. The rendering work is split into chunks, by individual route segments ([layouts and pages](/docs/app/getting-started/layouts-and-pages)), including [parallel route slots](/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/parallel-routes) whether or not they are displayed:\\n107|\\n108|- **Server Components** are rendered into a special data format called the React Server Component Payload (RSC Payload).\\n109|- **Client Components** and the RSC Payload are used to [prerender](/docs/app/glossary#prerendering) HTML.\\n110|\\n111|> **What is the React Server Component Payload (RSC)?**\\n112|>\\n113|> The RSC Payload is a compact, serialized representation of the rendered React Server Components tree. It's used by React on the client to update the browser's DOM. The RSC Payload contains:\\n114|>\\n115|> - The rendered result of Server Components\\n116|> - Placeholders for where Client Components ...", "duration_ms": 22, "is_error": false, "timestamp": 1791119398014}
